-- title:
--   Theorem 5.8 — existence of polynomial-size witnesses
-- statement:
--   There is a single polynomial bound with the following property. Consider a game $G$ with a reward function $r$ (rational transition probabilities and rewards, each defined using at most $B_{\mathrm{inp}}$ bits), a succinct discount factor $\lambda=1-2^{-2^{B_\lambda}}$, and additive error $\varepsilon=2^{-B_\varepsilon}$. Then there exists a witness $(\sigma,\tau,I_\sigma,I_\tau)$ whose size is polynomial in $n$, $m$, $B_{\mathrm{inp}}$, $B_\varepsilon$ and $B_\lambda$:
--   $$\mathrm{size}(\sigma,\tau,I_\sigma,I_\tau)\le(n+m+B_{\mathrm{inp}}+B_\varepsilon+B_\lambda+1)^k$$
--   for an exponent $k$ fixed before the game.
--
--   This is the completeness of witnesses: every instance has a short certificate.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - encoding: a finite game G = (S, A, B, δ) with |S| = n and at most m actions per player → states Fin n, actions Fin mA and Fin mB with n, mA, mB ≥ 1, m = max(mA, mB), distributions as nonnegative real vectors summing to one; reason: relabelling of finite sets; nonempty state and action sets are the source game convention
--   - encoding: the probability measure on valid plays Ω_s generated by finite histories, strategies on valid histories → the Ionescu-Tulcea trajectory measure on all sequences in (S × A × B)^ℕ; strategies are defined on all finite stage lists; reason: invalid plays have probability zero and values of strategies on invalid histories never matter, so every expectation and value is unchanged
--   - encoding: size = number of bits of the power-series representation (ℓ(B + 1) bits per number); "polynomial in n, m, B_inp, Bε, Bλ" → size = length of the self-delimiting binary encoding of the tuple; a bound (n + m + B_inp + Bε + Bλ + 1)^k with one exponent k chosen before all instances; reason: the two size measures are polynomially related; every polynomial in the five parameters is bounded by such a power and conversely
--   - encoding: rationals defined using at most B_inp bits; ε = 2^{-Bε} ∈ (0, 1) → numerator bits plus denominator bits at most B_inp; valid instances have Bε ≥ 1; reason: standing conventions of §2
-- source:
--   Ali Asadi, Krishnendu Chatterjee, The Computational Complexity of Concurrent Stochastic Games: Exact Value and Approximation, STOC 2027 submission #285 (October 2026), SHA-256 5cff187cf6b230b3bdfe540f2832f824d1ddb6acb43239fbf469d37c43b0d461, https://github.com/sigact-stoc/stoc2027/releases/download/papers-1/285.pdf, Theorem 5.8, printed p. 15 (PDF p. 17)

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_ConcurrentStochasticApprox_Approx_Game
import Definitions.Def_ConcurrentStochasticApprox_Approx_PowerSeries
import Definitions.Def_ConcurrentStochasticApprox_Approx_Problems
open Finset

namespace ConcurrentStochasticApprox.Approx

/-- **Theorem 5.8 (Existence of polynomial-size witness).** There is one polynomial bound such that
for every game `G` with reward `r` (rational data defined using at most `Binp` bits), succinct
discount factor `λ = 1 - 2^{-2^{Bλ}}` and additive error `ε = 2^{-Bε}`, a witness
`(σ, τ, I_σ, I_τ)` exists whose size is at most that polynomial in `n, m, Binp, Bε, Bλ`. -/
theorem theorem_5_8 :
    ∃ k : ℕ, ∀ (I : DiscInst) (hI : I.Valid) (Binp : ℕ), I.BitsLE Binp →
      ∃ W : WitnessData I.G.n I.G.mA I.G.mB,
        IsWitness (I.G.toCSG hI.1) I.r.toReal I.Blam I.Bε W ∧
        W.enc.length ≤ (I.G.n + I.G.m + Binp + I.Bε + I.Blam + 1) ^ k := by sorry

end ConcurrentStochasticApprox.Approx
